```
ret = nand_ecc_register_on_host_hw_engine(&sfc->ecc_eng);
if (ret)
    return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, ret, "failed to register Aml host ecc engine.\n");
```
Reason:  
The ECC engine is registered but never unregistered on probe failures after this point (e.g., later `devm_spi_register_controller` failure) or on device removal. This leaks the ECC engine resource and can cause a use-after-free when the SPI controller is gone but the engine remains registered.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
static void aml_sfc_unregister_ecc_engine(void *data)
{
    struct nand_ecc_engine *eng = data;
    nand_ecc_unregister_on_host_hw_engine(eng);
}
```
and
```
ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, aml_sfc_unregister_ecc_engine,
                               &sfc->ecc_eng);
if (ret)
    return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "failed to add ECC unregister action\n");
```
Reason:  
The `devm_add_action_or_reset` registers a devres cleanup action that calls `aml_sfc_unregister_ecc_engine`, which in turn calls `nand_ecc_unregister_on_host_hw_engine`. This ensures the ECC engine is automatically unregistered both when probe fails after the action is added and when the device is removed, preventing the leak and potential use-after-free.

